\details{
\subsection{Interpreting results}{

Low-count outliers are expected and normal for narrow syndrome definitions,
particularly at small rural hospitals. A rural ED with two opioid overdose
visits in a given month is not a data quality problem; it may reflect
true low incidence. This function is best used on \strong{denominator data}
(all-cause ED visits) where facility volumes are stable and comparable.
When applied to narrow case definitions, some ED facilities will routinely
appear as low outliers. The function is intended to make routine QA
assessment easier, not to drive exclusion decisions. Always review
flagged facilities in context.
}

\subsection{Outlier methods}{

\describe{
\item{\code{"percentile"} (default)}{Flags facilities below
\code{percentile_low} and above \code{percentile_high} of the visit count
distribution. This is a tail ranking, not a test for unusual values: it
flags roughly the most extreme \code{percentile_low + (1 - percentile_high)}
share of facilities whether or not any of them is actually abnormal
(with the default thresholds and fewer than about 20 facilities it
flags the lowest and the highest facility every time). Use it to
produce a short, ordered list to look at; use \code{"iqr"} when you want
only facilities that are unusual relative to the spread of the rest.}
\item{\code{"iqr"}}{Flags facilities below \code{Q1 - iqr_multiplier * IQR} and
above \code{Q3 + iqr_multiplier * IQR}. Tukey fences with the default
multiplier of 1.5 flag nothing when facilities are similar, and are
sensitive to data dropouts: a facility that abruptly stops sending
visits during a pull window will fall well below the lower fence.
Increase \code{iqr_multiplier} to reduce sensitivity.}
\item{\code{"both"}}{Applies both methods independently. A facility is
flagged if it meets either criterion. \code{.outlier_method} records which
criterion fired: \code{"percentile"}, \code{"iqr"}, or \code{"percentile+iqr"}.}
}
}

\subsection{Time normalization}{

When \code{date_col} is supplied, visits per day is computed and outlier
detection is applied to that rate rather than raw counts.
\code{visits_per_day} is a facility's \code{n_visits} divided by the number of
distinct dates in the whole pull, not by the days that facility sent data
(\code{n_days}, reported alongside for reference). A facility that sends data
every day of a 30-day pull and one that sends the same daily volume for 10
days and then stops therefore get different rates, as they get different
counts.

Every facility is divided by the same number of days, so the rate is a
rescaling of the count and ranks the facilities identically: the
facilities flagged are the same as with raw counts. What \code{date_col} adds
is the unit (visits per day), which lets you compare the rates of pulls
of different durations. A dropout is flagged by either, because a
facility that stops sending has a low count.
}

\subsection{Grouped detection}{

When \code{group_by_type = TRUE}, outlier thresholds are computed separately
within each \code{FacilityType}. This prevents large academic medical centers
from inflating thresholds that mask low counts at smaller facilities.
}
